\begin{abstract}
Reinforcement learning turns autoregressive language models into reasoners by treating each generated token as a discrete action with an explicit probability and pushing the probabilities of rewarded tokens up. Continuous flow language models have no such actions: they denoise a whole sequence of token embeddings from Gaussian noise over a number of flow steps and read out tokens only at the end, so a sample is a differentiable function of the noise rather than a sequence of decisions. We show that porting the autoregressive recipe to this setting is structurally the wrong choice: the likelihood-ratio gradient of a denoising step is the injected noise itself, so a reward bit is spread over the enormous set of noise coordinates of a trajectory, none of which decides a token, and the signal vanishes however the advantage is refined. We propose a post-training framework built on what a flow model has instead, differentiability. Its core is a \hl{two-dimensional critic}: a learned value over the two axes along which credit lives in a flow model, the \emph{flow step} (when, along the denoising trajectory, a state is evaluated) and the \emph{token position} (where, along the sequence, the outcome is decided). The critic scores every token position of any intermediate noisy state; its pooled value is optimised by back-propagating through the denoiser at a random flow step, and it is trained jointly with the policy from verifier outcomes under the deployment sampler. An amortisation stage then distils the policy's verified samples back into the model so that cheaper samplers inherit the gains. On four verifiable reasoning tasks this is, to our knowledge, the first demonstration that reinforcement learning reliably improves a continuous flow language model, where ported score-function methods give small or unstable gains.
\end{abstract}
